 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "O'CONNELL BRIDGE. Wider than it is long. It started as a narrow stone bridge, but it was widened in 1880 to match the street, so people could say it looked square. It does."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "THE RIVER LIFFEY. Dublin comes from 'Dubh Linn', the black pool. The Irish name is Baile \u00c1tha Cliath, 'the town of the hurdle ford'. Both names are about crossing water. Wise."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "THE HA'PENNY BRIDGE. A cast iron footbridge from 1816 that used to cost a halfpenny to cross. The toll went in 1919. The name stuck, which is how you can tell Dubliners love a bargain."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "THE PARK. Dublin has one of the biggest walled city parks in Europe, with a herd of fallow deer that have lived there since the 1660s. The park's name comes from 'fionn uisce', clear water. It was mangled into 'phoenix' a long time ago."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "DUBLIN HALL. Where Dublin plays hurling and gets rather loud about it. It's the fastest field game in the world, played with a stick called a hurley and a ball called a sliotar. Helmets, please."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "TODAY ON THE STAGE: an announcement. Tote bags are free. The announcement is a surprise, and so are the tote bags."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "THE CUSTOM HOUSE. A grand Georgian building on the quays, finished in 1791, and burned by Irish rebels in 1921. It was restored. It is quite impressive when it isn't on fire."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 39,
   "text": "TRINITY COLLEGE. Founded in 1592, it holds the Book of Kells, a gospel manuscript made around the year 800, and the Long Room, a library hall 65 m long with about 200,000 old books and a smell students call 'academic'."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "THE CAMPANILE. Legend says that if you walk under it, you'll fail your exams. Students walk round it. They fail anyway, just more slowly."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 39,
   "text": "MOLLY MALONE. A bronze fishmonger with a barrow, from the old song, 'cockles and mussels, alive, alive-o'. She stands in a street full of buskers and has never once joined the chorus."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "TEMPLE BAR. Cobbled lanes, pubs, and live music from every window. The name is old, and 'bar' here means a walkway, not a pub, which no one has told the pubs."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "ST STEPHEN'S GREEN. 22 acres of lawn, flowerbeds and ducks, opened to the public in 1880 after Lord Ardilaun, of the Guinness family, paid for the layout. The ducks have never said thank you."
  },
  {
   "x": 61,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "DUBLIN PORT. The ferry for Holyhead leaves from here. The crossing is a little over three hours, the sea is grey, and the chips on board are optimistic."
  }
 ],
